From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dma-buf: heaps: cma: map a whole buffer at mmap time

A CMA heap buffer is physically contiguous, yet mmap() maps it one page per
fault. On the SG2002 (C906) the faults cost about 13 us a page: filling a
freshly mapped 60 MiB buffer took 390 ms, 185 ms with the whole buffer
mapped at once (a second pass 20 ms either way). The TPU runtime fills
such buffers with the weights and command buffers of every model it loads.

Map the requested range with remap_pfn_range() when the buffer is mapped.
The VMA flags are those remap_pfn_range() sets (VM_IO | VM_PFNMAP |
VM_DONTEXPAND | VM_DONTDUMP), as before. The dma-buf core already refuses
a range past the buffer; the heap checks it again before mapping.

Registering a model from memory on the NanoKVM: the 20 MB Latin text
recognizer 300 -> 255 ms, the 24 MB Chinese one 353 -> 268 ms, a 4 MB one
59 -> 49 ms; the 26 MB text detector unchanged (440 ms).

Map writable buffers dirty too (formerly 0067):
The C906 does not set the accessed and dirty bits of a PTE itself: a store
to a page whose PTE lacks D raises a page fault, and the kernel sets the bit.
Mapped whole at mmap time, a buffer had the protection of a shared
writable mapping, which on RISC-V has A but not D. Every first store
to a page of a buffer therefore still faulted: a TPU model load wrote its
weights into such buffers at about 12 us a page.

Map a writable buffer dirty. Nothing tracks the dirty pages of this PFN map
(no page cache, no writeback), so the bit only spares the fault.

Signed-off-by: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/dma-buf/heaps/cma_heap.c | 42 +++++++++++++++++---------------
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 20 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/cma_heap.c b/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/cma_heap.c
index 3fb4b946c91a..078d19fdcb49 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/cma_heap.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/cma_heap.c
@@ -166,21 +166,6 @@ static int cma_heap_dma_buf_end_cpu_access(struct dma_buf *dmabuf,
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static vm_fault_t cma_heap_vm_fault(struct vm_fault *vmf)
-{
-	struct vm_area_struct *vma = vmf->vma;
-	struct cma_heap_buffer *buffer = vma->vm_private_data;
-
-	if (vmf->pgoff >= buffer->pagecount)
-		return VM_FAULT_SIGBUS;
-
-	return vmf_insert_pfn(vma, vmf->address, page_to_pfn(buffer->pages[vmf->pgoff]));
-}
-
-static const struct vm_operations_struct dma_heap_vm_ops = {
-	.fault = cma_heap_vm_fault,
-};
-
 static int cma_heap_mmap(struct dma_buf *dmabuf, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
 {
 	struct cma_heap_buffer *buffer = dmabuf->priv;
@@ -188,12 +173,29 @@ static int cma_heap_mmap(struct dma_buf *dmabuf, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
 	if ((vma->vm_flags & (VM_SHARED | VM_MAYSHARE)) == 0)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	vm_flags_set(vma, VM_IO | VM_PFNMAP | VM_DONTEXPAND | VM_DONTDUMP);
-
-	vma->vm_ops = &dma_heap_vm_ops;
-	vma->vm_private_data = buffer;
+	if (vma->vm_pgoff + vma_pages(vma) > buffer->pagecount)
+		return -EINVAL;
 
-	return 0;
+	/*
+	 * The buffer is physically contiguous: map all of it now. Inserting
+	 * one page per fault cost about 13 us a page on a C906, 0.2 s for
+	 * each 60 MiB a TPU model writes.
+	 */
+	pgprot_t prot = vma->vm_page_prot;
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_RISCV
+	/*
+	 * Writable, map it dirty too. A core that does not set the A and D bits
+	 * itself (the C906) faults on the first store to a page whose PTE lacks
+	 * D, and the kernel only sets the bit: about 12 us a page, the same
+	 * 0.2 s for 60 MiB. Nothing tracks dirty pages of this PFN map.
+	 */
+	if (vma->vm_flags & VM_WRITE)
+		prot = __pgprot(pgprot_val(prot) | _PAGE_DIRTY);
+#endif
+	return remap_pfn_range(vma, vma->vm_start,
+			       page_to_pfn(buffer->cma_pages) + vma->vm_pgoff,
+			       vma->vm_end - vma->vm_start, prot);
 }
 
 static void *cma_heap_do_vmap(struct cma_heap_buffer *buffer)
